## Migrating from hpegl

This data source is the hpe equivalent of `hpegl_vmaas_instance_storage_controller`. hpegl's
`id` is the composed mount string (for example `-1:1:6:0`), which is exactly hpe's
`controller_mount_point` format `id:busNumber:typeId:unitNumber`. This data source exposes
both the controller type `id` and the composed `controller_mount_point`, so it is a superset.

```hcl
# hpegl
data "hpegl_vmaas_instance_storage_controller" "scsi_0" {
  name             = "SCSI VMware Paravirtual"
  bus_number       = 1
  interface_number = 0
}

# hpe
data "hpe_morpheus_storage_controller_type" "scsi_0" {
  controller_name  = "SCSI VMware Paravirtual"
  bus_number       = 1
  interface_number = 0
}

resource "hpe_morpheus_instance" "example" {
  volumes = [{
    controller_mount_point = data.hpe_morpheus_storage_controller_type.scsi_0.controller_mount_point
  }]
}
```

hpegl's `layout_id` has no equivalent argument: the Morpheus option source resolves the
layout to its provision type and reads the same controller list, so layout scoping is
incidental. Use `provision_type_code` (defaulting to `vmware`) instead.

The raw form is still available, which is useful on older provider versions:

```hcl
locals { scsi_paravirtual = 6 } # from /api/provision-types?code=vmware
volumes = [{
  controller_mount_point = format("-1:%d:%d:%d", 1, local.scsi_paravirtual, 0)
}]
```

